        'Netherlands',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1800s: Jass games spread through the Netherlands and Belgium',
        'Late 1800s: Klaverjassen establishes itself as the Dutch national card game',
        '1900s: Regional variants develop in Amsterdam, Rotterdam, and Utrecht',
        'Present: Remains extremely popular in Dutch cafes and social clubs; played in organized competitions',
      ],
      evolution: "The game evolved distinct Dutch characteristics including the 'roem' (melds) system and specific trump-choosing conventions. The Amsterdam, Rotterdam, and Utrecht variants differ mainly in rules about when trumps must be played.",
      cultural: 'Klaverjassen is deeply embedded in Dutch cafÃ© culture. It is one of the few games where signaling conventions (within strict rules) are an accepted part of play. The game is often played in social clubs and at family gatherings.',
