        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Score as many points as possible over 16 hands. In each hand, the team that chooses trumps must collect more than half the available points (81+ out of 162). If they fail, the opposing team gets all the points.',
          gameplay: "Player to dealer's left leads to first trick. Must follow suit if possible. Amsterdam rules: If cannot follow and opponent is winning, must beat with trump if possible. If partner is winning, may play any card (except cannot undertrump). Rotterdam rules: Must always play trump if cannot follow suit; must beat any trumps already played if possible.",
          keyRules: [
            'Trump ranking: Jack (20), Nine (14), Ace (11), Ten (10), King (4), Queen (3), Eight (0), Seven (0)',
            'Plain suit ranking: Ace (11), Ten (10), King (4), Queen (3), Jack (2), Nine (0), Eight (0), Seven (0)',
            'Must follow suit, must trump if cannot follow (Rotterdam)',
            'Roem (melds): sequences and four-of-a-kinds score bonus points',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'The Jack and Nine of trumps are the most powerful cards (34 points together). If you have these, you have strong control of the trump suit. Always try to establish how many trumps your partner holds.',
          intermediate: 'Roem (melds) can significantly affect the score. A run of 3 (20 points) or 4 (50 points) can swing a hand. Be careful not to give opponents the cards they need for their melds. The Stuk (trump King and Queen) is worth 20 points and should be protected.',
          advanced: 'Signaling: Partners use legal signals to communicate hand strength. A high-low signal in a suit suggests an even number of cards; low-high suggests odd. Master the Amsterdam vs Rotterdam rules about when you must trumpâ€”this affects endgame play significantly.',
          tips: [
            'Remember the two different card orders: trick-taking order (Jack, Nine high in trumps) vs meld sequence order (Ace high)',
            'The last trick is worth 10 pointsâ€”plan accordingly',
            'If you choose trumps, you need 82+ points to score; aim for at least 90 to be safe',
            'Four Jacks is worth 200 pointsâ€”nearly enough to win a hand by itself',
            "In Amsterdam rules, you can 'throw off' when partner is winning; in Rotterdam, you must always trump if possible",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
